Short answer
- Use a single strict Zod object for the str_replace variant and enforce “exactly one convention” with a superRefine that implements XOR of the two key-pairs. This keeps the schema compatible with z.discriminatedUnion('command', …) (which requires each variant to be a ZodObject) and avoids .passthrough().
- Do not try to make the top-level str_replace variant itself a union; z.discriminatedUnion does not accept unions/effects as options.
- You can add an optional narrowing helper or a post-parse normalizer for ergonomic typing without any as any casts.

Complete solution

1) StrReplace schema that enforces “exactly one naming convention,” rejects mixes, and is strict

```ts
import { z } from 'zod';

// Optional: if you want non-empty paths, add .min(1)
const PathSchema = z.string();

export const StrReplaceCommandSchema = z
  .object({
    command: z.literal('str_replace'),
    path: PathSchema,

    // Both naming styles are declared optional. We enforce the XOR + completeness with superRefine.
    old_str: z.string().optional(),
    new_str: z.string().optional(),

    old_string: z.string().optional(),
    new_string: z.string().optional(),
  })
  .strict() // reject any extra, unknown keys (no passthrough!)
  .superRefine((data, ctx) => {
    // Detect what was actually supplied (as strings). Optional properties that are not present are undefined.
    const hasSnakeOld = typeof data.old_str === 'string';
    const hasSnakeNew = typeof data.new_str === 'string';
    const hasOldOld = typeof data.old_string === 'string';
    const hasOldNew = typeof data.new_string === 'string';

    const anySnake = hasSnakeOld || hasSnakeNew;
    const anyOld = hasOldOld || hasOldNew;

    // 1) Mixing conventions is not allowed
    if (anySnake && anyOld) {
      if (hasSnakeOld)
        ctx.addIssue({
          code: z.ZodIssueCode.custom,
          path: ['old_str'],
          message:
            'Do not mix naming conventions. Use old_str/new_str OR old_string/new_string, not both.',
        });
      if (hasSnakeNew)
        ctx.addIssue({
          code: z.ZodIssueCode.custom,
          path: ['new_str'],
          message:
            'Do not mix naming conventions. Use old_str/new_str OR old_string/new_string, not both.',
        });
      if (hasOldOld)
        ctx.addIssue({
          code: z.ZodIssueCode.custom,
          path: ['old_string'],
          message:
            'Do not mix naming conventions. Use old_str/new_str OR old_string/new_string, not both.',
        });
      if (hasOldNew)
        ctx.addIssue({
          code: z.ZodIssueCode.custom,
          path: ['new_string'],
          message:
            'Do not mix naming conventions. Use old_str/new_str OR old_string/new_string, not both.',
        });
      return;
    }

    // 2) Neither convention provided
    if (!anySnake && !anyOld) {
      ctx.addIssue({
        code: z.ZodIssueCode.custom,
        path: ['old_str'],
        message:
          'Provide either old_str & new_str OR old_string & new_string.',
      });
      ctx.addIssue({
        code: z.ZodIssueCode.custom,
        path: ['old_string'],
        message:
          'Provide either old_str & new_str OR old_string & new_string.',
      });
      return;
    }

    // 3) If snake_case selected, both keys must be present
    if (anySnake) {
      if (!hasSnakeOld) {
        ctx.addIssue({
          code: z.ZodIssueCode.custom,
          path: ['old_str'],
          message: 'old_str is required when using snake_case.',
        });
      }
      if (!hasSnakeNew) {
        ctx.addIssue({
          code: z.ZodIssueCode.custom,
          path: ['new_str'],
          message: 'new_str is required when using snake_case.',
        });
      }
      return; // done
    }

    // 4) If old_string style selected, both keys must be present
    if (anyOld) {
      if (!hasOldOld) {
        ctx.addIssue({
          code: z.ZodIssueCode.custom,
          path: ['old_string'],
          message: 'old_string is required when using old_string/new_string style.',
        });
      }
      if (!hasOldNew) {
        ctx.addIssue({
          code: z.ZodIssueCode.custom,
          path: ['new_string'],
          message: 'new_string is required when using old_string/new_string style.',
        });
      }
    }
  });
```

Why this works
- .strict() ensures no extra fields get through (fixes the .passthrough() security issue).
- The superRefine enforces one-and-only-one pair of keys, complete as a pair, never mixed.
- The schema is a single ZodObject with command: 'str_replace', so it can be used inside a discriminatedUnion('command', …) without violating Zod’s requirements.

2) Integration into your discriminated union

```ts
const MemoryCommandSchema = z.discriminatedUnion('command', [
  ViewCommandSchema,
  CreateCommandSchema,
  StrReplaceCommandSchema, // fixed one from above
  InsertCommandSchema,
  DeleteCommandSchema,
  RenameCommandSchema,
]);
```

3) Types you get out and how to keep them ergonomic without casts

By default, z.infer<typeof StrReplaceCommandSchema> will have four optional fields (old_str, new_str, old_string, new_string). This is type-safe, but the union-ness is not represented at the type level. To work ergonomically without any as any:

- Provide type guards to narrow which convention you have at runtime.
- Optionally provide a small normalizer function that converts the old_string/new_string variant to a single canonical shape you use internally.

Type guards (no casts):

```ts
type StrReplaceRaw = z.infer<typeof StrReplaceCommandSchema>;

export function isSnakeCaseReplace(x: StrReplaceRaw): x is StrReplaceRaw & {
  old_str: string;
  new_str: string;
} {
  return typeof x.old_str === 'string' && typeof x.new_str === 'string';
}

export function isOldStringReplace(x: StrReplaceRaw): x is StrReplaceRaw & {
  old_string: string;
  new_string: string;
} {
  return (
    typeof x.old_string === 'string' && typeof x.new_string === 'string'
  );
}
```

Optional: normalization helper (no casts):

```ts
export type StrReplaceNormalized = {
  command: 'str_replace';
  path: string;
  old_str: string;
  new_str: string;
};

export function normalizeStrReplace(x: StrReplaceRaw): StrReplaceNormalized {
  if (isSnakeCaseReplace(x)) {
    return {
      command: x.command,
      path: x.path,
      old_str: x.old_str,
      new_str: x.new_str,
    };
  }
  // If we reach here, by schema it must be the old_string/new_string style
  return {
    command: x.command,
    path: x.path,
    old_str: x.old_string!,
    new_str: x.new_string!,
  };
}
```

4) Answers to your specific questions

- What’s the correct Zod pattern to enforce “exactly one naming convention per call” within a discriminated union?
  - Use a single strict ZodObject for the str_replace variant with optional keys for both conventions and a superRefine that enforces XOR semantics. This keeps it compatible with z.discriminatedUnion('command', …) while preventing mixing and extra keys.

- Can we use union types for the properties themselves? Or custom refinements?
  - You can’t put a union-of-objects directly as the str_replace option of a discriminatedUnion because z.discriminatedUnion requires each option to be a ZodObject (not a union/effect). The idiomatic approach is to use one object plus a custom superRefine (or an external XOR helper) to enforce mutual exclusivity.

- How do we maintain type safety without as any casts?
  - The schema itself is fully type-safe and strict. For ergonomic usage, add small type guards (no casts needed) to narrow to one variant or provide a normalizer function that returns a single canonical shape you use internally. Both approaches avoid any as any.

- Is there a way to make the discriminated union work with conditional schemas?
  - Not directly. z.discriminatedUnion options must be ZodObject with distinct discriminator literals. You can’t pass a union or an effect as an option. Put your conditional logic inside the object (via superRefine) or normalize after parsing.

Validation behavior examples

- Valid:
  - {command: 'str_replace', path: '/memories/file', old_str: 'text1', new_str: 'text2'}
  - {command: 'str_replace', path: '/memories/file', old_string: 'text1', new_string: 'text2'}

- Invalid (mixing):
  - {command: 'str_replace', path: '/memories/file', old_str: 'text', new_string: 'text2'}
  - {command: 'str_replace', path: '/memories/file', old_string: 'text', new_str: 'text2'}

- Invalid (incomplete pair):
  - {command: 'str_replace', path: '/memories/file', old_str: 'x'} // missing new_str
  - {command: 'str_replace', path: '/memories/file', new_string: 'y'} // missing old_string

- Invalid (extra fields due to strict):
  - {command: 'str_replace', path: '/memories/file', old_str: 'x', new_str: 'y', foo: 'bar'} // extra key rejected

This pattern satisfies the security constraint (no extra fields), backward compatibility (both naming styles accepted), and strict correctness (no mixing), while remaining compatible with your discriminated union and preserving type safety without any as any casts.
